  "articleBody": "Adding a bathroom creates wonderful convenience, but jumping in without evaluating hidden structural needs can quickly stall your build. Avoiding common planning oversights keeps your timeline intact and your budget predictable.\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid\n1. Underestimating drain and vent work: Tying into existing waste lines requires proper slope and venting. Forgetting to verify pipe capacity early often leads to torn-up flooring later.\n2. Skipping floor structure checks: Water-filled bathtubs and heavy tile add major dead weight. Floor joists must be evaluated to ensure they can carry the load without sagging.\n3. Placing fixtures without clearance checks: Codes mandate specific clearances around toilets, vanities, and shower entries. Cramming fixtures into tight spots leads to failed rough inspections.\n4. Omitting outside ventilation: Relying solely on interior airflow invites mold. Installing an outside-vented exhaust fan is vital for moisture control.\n5. Starting work before approvals: Beginning framing or rough-in plumbing without local permits risks costly stop-work orders and teardowns.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\nA qualified general contractor handles framing, waterproofing, and tile while coordinating licensed trades for plumbing and electrical lines. To review your bathroom addition plans, reach out to HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
